+#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[An invertible nonlinear-map benchmark]
+
+The earlier scalar task, $z_(t+1) = (z_t^2 + c_t) mod 256$, admits exact recovery
+from a parity summary of the omitted prefix plus the last seven drivers.
+Parity updates as $(z + c) mod 2$. Two states of equal parity have an
+even difference; each shared squaring step multiplies that difference
+by their even sum. Seven steps therefore erase the difference modulo 256.
+This task property retires the earlier accuracy comparison as a test of
+deep nonlinear composition. It does not identify which shortcut,
+if any, the trained models used.
+
+The replacement tracks two coordinates over the field with $p = 61$:
+$
+  F_(c)(x,y) = (y, x + y^2 + c) mod p,
+  quad F_c^(-1)(u,v) = (v - u^2 - c, u) mod p.
+$
+Each fixed-driver map is a permutation of all $p^2 = 3721$ states.
+Distinct full states never merge under any shared driver sequence.
+Uniform initial coordinates are given once in two typed prefix tokens;
+iid uniform field-valued drivers follow. At every driver position the
+model predicts the joint class $p x + y$ from the inputs alone. Previous
+targets and model predictions are never fed back. Training uses 128
+drivers; extrapolation uses 512 and 1024, with two prefix tokens at each
+length. Accuracy scores the complete state, rather than one coordinate.
+
+A predictor observing only the initialization and a bounded suffix of
+$k$ drivers has full-state accuracy $1 / p^2 = 0.0269%$ once at least
+two drivers are hidden before that suffix. To see this, two fresh drivers
+$a,b$ send any fixed state to $(u,v)$ with
+$a = u - x - y^2$ and $b = v - y - u^2$. Every target pair has exactly
+one driver pair, so the hidden state is uniform; subsequent known
+permutations preserve uniformity. The bound excludes the first $k+1$
+driver positions and predictors retaining additional prefix summaries.
+A recurrent model can retain the history beyond this observer class.
+We report accuracy after the first eight drivers separately for $k=7$.
+The named marginal-optimal loss reference is $ln(3721) = 8.221748$ nats,
+the cross-entropy of a uniform prediction over the joint state classes.
+
