+#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Tuned small models, difficulty, and local arithmetic]
+
+The nominal 8 M grid trains from scratch (measured parameters
+9,900,800 for E97 and 9,887,056 for GDN2), using the actual Gated
+DeltaNet-2 mixer with negative eigenvalues enabled. The symmetric grid
+uses learning rates 1e-3, 3e-4, and 1e-4, each with seeds 0/1/2,
+fresh procedural training, and validation-only checkpoint and rate
+selection. Both architectures select 1e-3 by mean validation accuracy.
+At that best grid point, test-1× accuracy is 0.0356 ± 0.0023% for
+E97 and 0.0493 ± 0.0112% for GDN2. At matched 3e-4 the means are
+0.0219% versus 0.0331%; at 1e-4 they are 0.0290% versus 0.0381%.
+GDN2 is at least as accurate as E97 in all nine primary matched
+learning-rate/seed test-1× cells. This scope is the primary matched
+cells, not every metric or post-seven-driver comparison. Both models'
+post-seven-driver and extrapolation results remain near the suffix
+reference. These task-sized-vocabulary Schedule-Free scratch runs and
+the pretrained full-vocabulary AdamW fine-tunes are different experiment
+classes; their raw scale comparison includes more than architecture.
+
+The seed-0 difficulty ladder reduces the field size. At matched 1e-3,
+$p=13$ gives 3.006% versus 4.538% test accuracy and $p=31$ gives
+0.786% versus 1.666%, for E97 and GDN2 respectively. E97's best
+validation-selected rate is 1e-3 for both; GDN2's is 3e-4 at $p=13$
+(6.038% test accuracy) and 1e-3 at $p=31$. Easier fields yield more
+signal, while accuracy after seven drivers stays near the corresponding
+suffix references (0.5917% and 0.1041%).
+
+At matched 1e-3, the seed-0 one-step diagnostics reach 97.66% for E97
+(500/512) and 100% for GDN2 (512/512). The local arithmetic is learnable;
+robust 128-deep composition is not learned at these scales by either
+class under the tested protocols. These late one-step successes do not
+certify arithmetic mastery at earlier long-task plateaus. The finite
+one-step input domain can also be revisited during procedural training,
+so this is an arithmetic/encoding diagnostic rather than a novel-input
+generalization certificate.
+
+#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Marginal-like behavior and limited signal]
+
+Losses stay close to the marginal-optimal reference, with a sliver below
+it: selected 1× cross-entropy means are 8.188895 for E97 and 8.223262
+for GDN2, against 8.221748 nats. E97's below-reference loss indicates
+limited predictive signal. Both arms' post-seven-driver accuracy stays
+near the suffix reference, and terminal full-state accuracy is zero in
+every seed at all three depths. Mean extrapolation losses are at or
+above the reference. We call this marginal-like loss behavior, not an
+identified internal marginal-solution mechanism or successful deep
+recurrence.
+
+Every finite-state task admits a sufficiently large one-hot linear-state
+representation; here each driver acts through a 3721-by-3721 permutation
+matrix. The benchmark tests learned efficiency at practical state sizes,
+not an impossibility for linear state. These findings establish neither
+architecture superiority in either direction nor a universal expressivity
+separation. Optimization, state size, pretraining, and selection remain
+part of the empirical question.
